.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt CONCHA 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm concha
.Nd a Unix shell whose scripting language is Pascal
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl il
.Nm
.Ar script
.Op Ar arg ...
.Nm
.Fl c
.Ar text
.Op Ar arg ...
.Nm
.Fl n
.Op Ar script
.Nm
.Fl q Cm check | Cm outline
.Ar script
.Nm
.Fl q Cm words | Cm def
.Ar script : Ns Ar line : Ns Ar column
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A tour for people who already use a Unix shell is
.Xr concha-tutorial 7 .
This page is the complete reference.
.Pp
.Nm
runs commands the way the Bourne shell does: pipes, redirections,
heredocs,
.Cm && ,
.Cm ||
and
.Cm & .
Everything else is Pascal: variables, expressions, control flow and
routines.
Values are never split into words or globbed a second time.
.Pp
With no arguments and a terminal on standard input,
.Nm
is interactive: it reads
.Pa ~/.concharc ,
then prompts for lines with editing and history
.Pq Sx "LINE EDITING" .
Given a
.Ar script ,
it runs that file.
With
.Fl c ,
it runs
.Ar text .
If standard input is not a terminal, it reads standard input as a
script.
In each case the remaining arguments go into the list
.Ev args .
.Pp
Terminals and login programs may put
.Fl l ,
.Fl i
or both, as
.Fl il ,
before any of these.
.Fl l
asks for a login shell, which
.Nm
always is: it reads
.Pa ~/.concharc
and no profile.
.Fl i
makes the shell interactive even when standard input is not a
terminal, without line editing.
A first line beginning with
.Cm #!
is ignored.
.Pp
A script is parsed completely before it runs, so a syntax error
anywhere means nothing runs.
With
.Fl n ,
.Nm
only parses
.Ar script ,
or standard input if there is none: it reports the first syntax error
and runs nothing, like
.Ql sh -n .
Its message gives the column as well as the line, counted in bytes
from 1, of the token the error is about
.Pq the opening quote of a string left open :
.Dl concha: deploy.cn:12:9: expected 'then'
An unknown routine is not a syntax error, so it goes unreported.
.Pp
.Fl q
asks a query about
.Ar script ,
or standard input if it is
.Ql - ,
for an editor or a language server to read.
Like
.Fl n ,
it opens nothing but
.Ar script
and runs nothing.
The queries are listed in
.Sx QUERIES .
.Sh STATEMENTS AND COMMANDS
Each statement is one of two kinds, and the start of the line decides
which:
.Bl -bullet
.It
a keyword
.Pq Cm begin , if , while , repeat , for , case , var , export , procedure , function , break , continue , exit ,
an assignment
.Pq Cm "x :=" No or Cm "x[i] :=" ,
a call written with a parenthesis right after the name
.Pq Cm f( ,
or
.Ic write ,
.Ic writeln
or
.Ic halt
standing alone:
the statement is
.Em Pascal ;
.It
anything else is a
.Em command .
.El
.Pp
A newline or
.Cm \&;
ends a command.
So does a bare, unquoted
.Cm end , else
or
.Cm until
that closes an open
.Cm begin , if , case
or
.Cm repeat ,
which lets commands sit inside Pascal:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
if exists('Makefile') then make else echo nothing to do
.Ed
.Pp
Where nothing is open for it to close, the word is an ordinary
argument, so
.Cm "echo the end"
prints
.Sq the end .
.Pp
A line ending in
.Cm \&| , \&|& , &&
or
.Cm \&|| ,
or a backslash before the newline, continues the command on the next
line.
Pascal statements may span lines freely, and the
.Cm \&;
between them is optional.
.Pp
Comments are
.Cm //
to the end of the line, or
.Cm "{ ... }" .
In a command,
.Cm //
starts a comment only at the beginning of a word, and braces are
ordinary characters, so
.Cm "find . -exec rm {} \e;"
works.
After a call or a statement, where no word can follow, braces are a
comment again.
.Sh COMMAND WORDS
A command is a list of words.
A word is built from any of these parts, joined with no spaces between
them:
.Bl -tag -width "`pipeline`XX"
.It Ar text
Literal.
The characters
.Cm "* ? [ ..."
make it a glob pattern.
.It Sq Ar text
A Pascal string, taken literally.
Write
.Cm \&''
for a quote.
.It Do Ar text Dc
An interpolated string: expands
.Cm $ Ns Ar name ,
.Cm ${ Ns Ar name Ns Cm }
and
.Cm $( Ns Ar expr Ns Cm \&) .
.Cm \e\(dq , \e\e , \e$
and
.Cm \e\&`
escape.
.It Cm $ Ns Ar name , Cm ${ Ns Ar name Ns Cm }
The value of a variable.
The braces end the name where a letter follows:
.Cm \(dq${HOME}x\(dq .
.It Cm $( Ns Ar expr Ns Cm \&)
The value of any expression.
.It Cm \&` Ns Ar list Ns Cm \&`
The standard output of a list of statements, separated by
.Cm \&;
or newlines, without trailing newlines.
The list runs in a child process, so its assignments do not reach the
shell.
.It Cm <( Ns Ar list Ns Cm \&)
Process substitution: a word of its own, with blanks or line ends on
both sides, never a part of a longer one:
.Cm "x<(true)"
is a syntax error, and
.Cm "3<(list)"
is no process substitution but an fd redirection, descriptor 3 read from
the file
.Pa (list) .
The list runs in a child process with its standard output on a pipe,
and the word becomes
.Pa /dev/fd/ Ns Ar n ,
the pipe's read end, for the command to open as a file:
.Cm "diff <(sort a) <(sort b)" .
It works for procedures, builtins and redirections alike
.Pq Cm "cat < <(ls)" .
Under job control the lists join the command's job, its process group,
so that they can read the terminal.
Once the command, or the pipeline it stands in, has finished, the read
end is closed and the child waited for; its status is not the
command's, and
.Cm errexit
ignores it.
Inside, an unquoted
.Cm \&)
ends a word, so quote one that is meant.
Only this input form exists; there is no
.Cm >( Ns Ar list Ns Cm \&) .
With a space,
.Cm "< (" ,
it is a redirection from a file whose name starts with
.Cm \&( .
.It Cm ~ , ~ Ns Ar user
At the start of a word, before a
.Cm /
or the end of the word:
the value of
.Ev HOME ,
or the home directory of
.Ar user
as
.Pa /etc/passwd
gives it.
When there is no such user, the word is left as it is.
As in
.Xr sh 1 ,
in a word that starts
.Ar name Ns Cm = ,
with
.Ar name
an identifier, the same holds right after that
.Cm =
and after any unquoted
.Cm \&: ,
where a
.Cm \&:
may also end it:
.Cm env CONFIG=~/.cfg cmd
and
.Cm PATH=~/bin:~/.local/bin
expand.
Quoted, or anywhere else in a word,
.Cm ~
is literal.
.It Cm \e Ns Ar c
The character
.Ar c ,
taken literally.
.El
.Pp
A word expands to exactly one argument, with one exception: a word
that is only an unquoted
.Cm $ Ns Ar name
or
.Cm $( Ns Ar expr Ns Cm \&)
whose value is a list becomes one argument per item.
Values are never re-split on blanks and never re-globbed.
Use
.Fn split
or
.Fn lines
when splitting is wanted.
.Pp
Globbing applies only to unquoted literal text.
Patterns use
.Cm "* ? [abc] [a-z]"
and
.Cm [!abc] .
Names starting with a dot match only a pattern that starts with a dot.
Matches are sorted.
A pattern that matches nothing is an error.
.Pp
The first word names the command.
.Nm
looks for, in order: a user procedure or function, a builtin command,
and then an executable along
.Ev PATH .
If
.Ev PATH
is unset or empty,
.Pa /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
is searched instead.
An executable file that the system cannot run, such as a script with
no
.Cm #!
line, is run by
.Pa /bin/sh .
Arguments reach external programs as strings: integers in decimal,
booleans as
.Cm true
or
.Cm false ,
.Cm nil
as the empty string.
.Sh REDIRECTION
.Bl -tag -width "n<file, n>file, n>>fileXX"
.It Cm < Ns Ar file
Standard input from
.Ar file .
.It Cm > Ns Ar file
Standard output to
.Ar file ,
truncating it.
.It Cm >> Ns Ar file
Standard output appended to
.Ar file .
.It Cm n< Ns Ar file , n> Ns Ar file , n>> Ns Ar file
The same for descriptor
.Ar n .
.It Cm n>& Ns Ar m
Descriptor
.Ar n
becomes a copy of
.Ar m ,
as in
.Cm 2>&1 .
Without
.Ar n
it is 1, so
.Cm >&2
sends standard output to standard error.
.It Cm &> Ns Ar file
Standard output and standard error to
.Ar file .
.It Cm <<< Ns Ar word
Standard input is
.Ar word
followed by a newline.
.It Cm << Ns Ar tag
A heredoc.
.El
.Pp
Write messages to standard error with
.Cm >&2 ,
as in
.Ql writeln('error') >&2 ,
not with
.Cm > Ns Pa /dev/stderr .
The second opens the file again, so when standard error is a file, as
under
.Ql 2> Ns Ar log
or
.Ql &> Ns Ar log ,
it empties it and writes over what was there; with
.Cm >>
the message itself is written over instead.
.Cm >&2
shares the descriptor, and its place in the file, with the shell.
.Xr sh 1
behaves the same way.
.Pp
A heredoc's body starts on the line after the command and ends at a
line holding only
.Ar tag .
The terminator may be indented: whatever indentation precedes it is
also removed from every line of the body.
The body expands
.Cm $ Ns Ar name ,
.Cm ${ Ns Ar name Ns Cm }
and
.Cm $( Ns Ar expr Ns Cm \&)
unless the tag is quoted
.Pq Cm <<'EOF' ,
in which case it is taken literally.
.Pp
Redirections apply to every kind of command, including builtins,
procedures and blocks:
.Cm "writeln(x) > f"
and
.Cm "begin ... end <<EOF"
both work.
A file that can't be opened prints the reason and sets
.Ev status
to 1; the script continues.
.Sh PIPELINES AND LISTS
.Bl -tag -width "a && bXXXX"
.It Ar a No \&| Ar b
Standard output of
.Ar a
to standard input of
.Ar b .
The status is that of
.Ar b ,
or see
.Ev pipefail .
.It Ar a No |& Ar b
Short for
.Ar a No 2>&1 | Ar b :
standard error goes down the pipe too.
.It Ar a No && Ar b
Run
.Ar b
if
.Ar a
succeeded.
.It Ar a No || Ar b
Run
.Ar b
if
.Ar a
failed.
.It Ar a No &
Run in the background.
Its process id goes into
.Ev lastpid .
It is a process group of its own, and
.Dv SIGHUP ,
.Dv SIGTERM ,
.Dv SIGUSR1
or
.Dv SIGUSR2
sent to that process reaches every command in it, so
.Ql kill $lastpid
stops a whole pipeline or block;
.Ql wait $lastpid
then gives 128 plus the signal.
Any other signal reaches only that process unless sent to the group,
as in
.Ql kill -KILL -- -$lastpid .
In an interactive shell it is a job, announced with its number and
process id, and again when it finishes or stops; see
.Sx JOB CONTROL .
In a script its standard input is
.Pa /dev/null .
.El
.Pp
A pipeline stage can be a command, a call
.Pq Cm f(x) ,
a
.Cm begin ... end
block, or an
.Cm if , while , for , repeat
or
.Cm case
statement:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
printf 'a\enb\en' | while readln(l) do writeln('> ', l)
begin echo b; echo a end | sort > sorted
.Ed
.Pp
A single stage runs inside the shell, except for external programs, so
procedures and blocks can change variables.
Stages of a longer pipeline each run in their own process.
.Sh VALUES
Every value is one of:
.Cm nil ,
a boolean
.Pq Cm true , false ,
a 64-bit integer, a string of bytes, or a list
.Pq Cm "[1, 'two', [3]]" .
Assignment copies; two variables never share a value.
.Pp
String literals are Pascal's:
.Cm 'it''s' ,
with
.Cm # Ns Ar n
for a character code, as in
.Cm 'a'#10'b' .
Strings index from 1
.Pq Cm s[1] ,
lists from 0
.Pq Cm l[0] .
.Sh EXPRESSIONS
Operators, from the highest precedence to the lowest (Pascal's
precedence):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
not, unary -
* div mod and
+ - or xor
= <> < > <= >= in
.Ed
.Pp
Comparisons therefore need parentheses around each side:
.Cm "(a = 1) and (b = 2)" .
.Pp
.Cm +
adds two integers, joins lists (a non-list operand is added as one
item), and otherwise concatenates the operands as strings.
.Cm - , * , div
and
.Cm mod
accept strings that hold numbers, so
.Cm "int(`wc -l < f`) + 1"
and
.Cm "`nproc` * 2"
both work.
There is no
.Cm / .
Integer arithmetic that would not fit in 64 bits, and a number too big
to hold, is an error,
.Dq integer overflow ,
never a wrapped result.
.Pp
.Cm =
compares values of the same kind.
An integer equals a string holding the same number, and
.Cm nil
equals the empty string.
Ordering compares numerically when either side is an integer, and
byte by byte for two strings.
.Ar x No in Ar list
tests membership, and
.Ar s No in Ar t
tests for a substring.
.Pp
.Cm and
and
.Cm or
short-circuit.
Conditions must be booleans: an integer or string condition is an
error, not a guess.
.Pp
.Cm \&` Ns Ar list Ns Cm \&`
in an expression gives the list's output as a string and sets
.Ev status
to the status of its last command.
.Cm \(dq...\(dq
interpolates just as it does in a command.
.Sh STATEMENTS
.Bd -literal -offset indent
var x := e, y
x := e
l[i] := e
begin statements end
if c then s else s
while c do s
repeat statements until c
for i := a to b do s
for i := a downto b do s
for x in list do s
case e of v1, v2: s; lo..hi: s else statements end
export NAME := e
export NAME
break
continue
exit
exit(e)
.Ed
.Pp
Pascal type annotations are accepted and ignored:
.Cm "var i: integer" .
.Cm otherwise
is a synonym for
.Cm else
in
.Cm case .
.Cm for No ... Cm in
requires a list, so use
.Cm "lines(`...`)"
to loop over the lines of output.
Case labels are compared with
.Cm = ;
a range
.Ar lo Ns Cm .. Ns Ar hi
matches when
.Ar lo No <= Ar e No <= Ar hi ,
ordered as by
.Cm < ,
and never matches a value that cannot be ordered against it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
case c of
  '0'..'9': writeln('digit');
  'a'..'z', '_': writeln('name')
end
.Ed
.Sh ROUTINES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
procedure greet(name, greeting);
begin
  writeln(greeting, ', ', name)
end;

function sq(x);
var y;
begin
  y := x * x;
  result := y
end;
.Ed
.Pp
A routine is defined when its definition runs, and defining it again
replaces it.
Calls can be Pascal style,
.Cm "greet('bob', 'hi')" ,
or command style,
.Cm "greet bob hi" .
A Pascal-style call to a name that is not a routine is an error,
.Dq unknown routine ,
naming the one close misspelling if there is exactly one
.Pq Dq did you mean ... ;
command style instead looks for a builtin command and then an
executable along
.Ev PATH ,
as described in
.Sx COMMAND WORDS .
If none is found, the error is
.Dq not found ;
at the interactive prompt it also names the one close misspelling among
builtin commands, procedures and programs along
.Ev PATH ,
if there is exactly one.
Command-style arguments are strings, except that a word that is just
.Cm $ Ns Ar name
passes the value unchanged.
Missing parameters are
.Cm nil .
Every call also receives the full argument list in the local variable
.Ev args .
.Pp
Inside a routine, new variables are local.
Assigning to an existing global changes the global.
Routines cannot see their callers' locals.
A function returns
.Ev result .
.Ic exit
leaves the routine.
.Cm exit( Ns Ar e Ns Cm )
sets
.Ev result
in a function and
.Ev status
anywhere else.
Recursion is limited to a depth of 400.
.Pp
Called as a command, a procedure's status is the last command status
it left; it starts at 0.
A procedure named
.Ic prompt
draws the interactive prompt.
.Sh VARIABLES AND ENVIRONMENT
The environment is imported at startup as exported global string
variables.
Only exported variables reach child processes, converted to strings.
Reading a variable that does not exist is an error, naming the one
close misspelling among locals and globals if there is exactly one;
test with
.Fn defined name .
An exported variable whose name is known only as the script runs is
read with
.Fn getenv ,
set with
.Fn setenv
and removed with
.Ic unset ;
.Fn environ
lists them.
.Pp
Variables the shell sets:
.Bl -tag -width "errexit, pipefail" -offset indent
.It Ev args
List of the script's arguments, or of the current routine's arguments.
.It Ev status
Exit status of the last command.
.It Ev errexit , pipefail
Both
.Cm false .
Set to
.Cm true
to stop on failure; see
.Sx STATUS .
.It Ev lastpid
Process id of the last background job, 0 before the first.
.It Ev pid
Process id of the shell, like
.Cm $$
in
.Xr sh 1 .
It is read-only, and a child process started by a capture or a
pipeline sees the same value.
.It Ev PWD
Current directory, updated by
.Ic cd .
.It Ev OLDPWD
Previous directory, set by
.Ic cd
and read by
.Cm "cd -" .
.It Ev black , red , green , yellow , blue , magenta , cyan , white , bold , dim , reset
Escape sequences for the terminal's basic colours, bold, dim and back
to normal, for use in a prompt:
.Dl write(red, status, reset, ' ')
When colour is off
.Pq Sx ENVIRONMENT
they are empty strings.
They are ordinary variables and may be reassigned.
.El
.Sh STATUS
After every command or pipeline,
.Ev status
holds its exit code: 0 for success, and 128+n if signal
.Ar n
killed it.
It is 127 when a command is not found and 126 when a command cannot be
executed.
Pascal statements leave it alone, but a call made as a statement is a
command: a builtin such as
.Fn writeln
has status 0, and a procedure the status of the last command statement
it ran, or 0 if it ran none.
A capture sets
.Ev status
to its command's, for the next statement to read.
.Pp
A failed command does not stop a script unless
.Ev errexit
is
.Cm true .
Then a command statement whose status is not 0 ends the script with
that status, saying which command it was and where, and running the
.Cm EXIT
trap; at the prompt it abandons the rest of the line.
Commands on the left of
.Cm &&
or
.Cm \&|| ,
including everything a procedure called there runs, and commands
inside captures are exempt.
Conditions are boolean expressions, not commands, so
.Cm if
and
.Cm while
need no exemption; test a command's status with
.Cm \&||
instead:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
errexit := true
grep -q TODO notes || echo 'nothing to do'
.Ed
.Pp
With
.Ev pipefail
set to
.Cm true ,
a pipeline's status is that of its last stage that failed, or 0 if
none did.
.Sh BUILTIN COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "source fileXX" -offset indent
.It Ic bg Op Ar job
Continue a stopped
.Ar job ,
the current one by default, in the background.
.It Ic cd Op Ar dir
Change directory, to
.Ev HOME
by default.
.Cm "cd -"
goes to
.Ev OLDPWD
and prints the new directory.
Each successful change sets
.Ev OLDPWD
to the previous
.Ev PWD .
A failure names the reason, as in
.Dl concha: cd: /root: permission denied
.It Ic disown Op Ar job
Forget
.Ar job ,
the current one by default: it leaves the
.Ic jobs
list, and the shell no longer passes
.Dv SIGHUP
on to it, so it outlives the terminal.
A stopped job is continued first.
.Ic wait
with no argument still waits for it, as it is still the shell's child.
.It Ic echo Op Fl n Ar arg No ...
Print the arguments separated by spaces.
With
.Fl n ,
no final newline.
.It Ic exec Op Ar command Op Ar arg ...
Replace the shell with
.Ar command ,
an external program, after applying the redirections.
If it cannot be run, the reason is printed,
.Ev status
is set to 127 (not found) or 126, and the shell carries on.
With no
.Ar command ,
the redirections apply to the shell itself from then on:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
exec 3> log
echo started >&3
.Ed
.It Ic fg Op Ar job
Bring
.Ar job ,
the current one by default, to the foreground, continuing it if it
was stopped, and wait for it.
.Ev status
is its status.
Only an interactive shell has jobs to bring back.
.It Ic help Op Ar name No ...
With no
.Ar name ,
list the builtin commands and routines and how each is used.
For a procedure or function, print its heading, the file and line it
was declared at, and the comment just above it: a run of
.Ql //
lines, or a
.Ql { ... }
that opens its own line.
For a builtin, print how it is used; for a keyword, point here.
On a terminal, keywords are bold, names blue and parameters cyan, as
the command line paints them, and where a procedure was declared is
magenta.
The status is 1 if any
.Ar name
is neither.
.It Ic jobs
List the jobs: number, process id, whether running or stopped, and
the command.
The current job is marked
.Sq + .
.It Ic source Ar file
Run
.Ar file
in the current shell.
.Ic exit
in it returns to the caller.
A file that can't be read is an error naming the reason.
Run by the
.Ic prompt
procedure, an error in
.Ar file
is shown and gives status 1, and the prompt carries on; anywhere else
it is an error like any other.
.It Ic trap Ar name signal No ...
Run the procedure
.Ar name
when one of the
.Ar signal Ns s
arrives.
Signals are named
.Cm HUP , INT , QUIT , TERM , USR1
and
.Cm USR2 ;
.Cm EXIT
means the shell is about to exit.
.Cm "trap - " Ns Ar signal No ...
restores the default, and
.Cm "trap '' " Ns Ar signal No ...
ignores the signal.
See
.Sx SIGNALS .
.It Ic type Ar name No ...
Say what each
.Ar name
is, a line for each thing it names: a keyword; then, in the order a
command looks for them, a procedure or function and where it was
declared, a builtin, and each program of that name along
.Ev PATH ,
so the first of these is what runs and the rest are shadowed by it;
and last a builtin routine, which a call names rather than a command.
A program that
.Ev PATH
reaches twice, through a symbolic link, is listed once.
On a terminal, a keyword and what kind of thing a name is are bold, a
name blue, a program's path green and where a procedure was declared
magenta; colour follows
.Ev NO_COLOR
and
.Ev TERM
as messages do.
The status is 1 if any
.Ar name
is none of them.
.It Ic ulimit Oo Fl c | f | n | s | t | v Oc Op Ar value | Cm unlimited
Print or set a soft resource limit for the shell and the commands it
starts:
.Fl c
core file size,
.Fl f
file size (the default),
.Fl s
stack size and
.Fl v
address space, all in kilobytes;
.Fl n
open files;
.Fl t
CPU seconds.
.It Ic umask Op Ar mode
Print the file creation mask as four octal digits, or set it to the
octal
.Ar mode .
Symbolic modes are not supported.
.It Ic unset Ar name No ...
Remove each variable: a local of the current routine if there is one,
else a global.
An exported variable leaves the environment too.
Naming a variable that does not exist is not an error.
.It Ic wait Op Ar pid No ...
Wait for all background jobs, or for each
.Ar pid ,
and set
.Ev status
to the status of the last one reaped: 0 if there was none, 127 for a
.Ar pid
that is not a child of the shell.
.El
.Sh BUILTIN ROUTINES
.Bl -tag -width "exists(p), isdir(p), isfile(p)XX" -offset indent
.It Fn write "e, ..."
Print the values with no separators.
.It Fn writeln "e, ..."
The same, followed by a newline.
A bare
.Ic writeln
prints only a newline.
.It Fn readln v
Read a line from standard input into the variable
.Ar v .
Returns false at end of input.
.It Fn inc "v[, n]" , Fn dec "v[, n]"
Add or subtract
.Ar n
(default 1).
.It Fn halt "[n]"
Exit the shell with status
.Ar n
(default: the current status).
.It Fn length x
Length of a string or list.
.It Fn copy "x, i[, n]"
Part of a string (from 1) or list (from 0).
.It Fn pos "sub, s"
Position of
.Ar sub
in
.Ar s ,
or 0.
.It Fn int x , Fn str x
Convert.
.It Fn chr n , Fn ord s
Character code conversions.
.It Fn upcase s , Fn lowcase s
Change ASCII case.
.It Fn replace "s, old, new"
.Ar s
with every
.Ar old
replaced by
.Ar new ,
scanning left to right.
An empty
.Ar old
is an error.
.It Fn trim s
Remove surrounding blanks.
.It Fn split "s[, sep]"
List of fields; with no separator, split on runs of blanks.
.It Fn join "l[, sep]"
One string, separated by a space by default.
.It Fn lines s
List of lines.
.It Fn sort l
A sorted copy of the list
.Ar l .
.It Fn glob pattern
Sorted list of matches, possibly empty.
.It Fn match "s, pattern"
Glob-match a string.
.It Fn exists p , Fn isdir p , Fn isfile p
Test a path.
.It Fn basename "p[, suffix]"
The last component of the path
.Ar p ,
trailing slashes ignored, less
.Ar suffix
if it ends with it and is not all of it, as
.Xr basename 1 :
.Ql "basename('/doc/a.html', '.html')"
is
.Ql a .
.It Fn dirname p
All of
.Ar p
but its last component, as
.Xr dirname 1 :
.Ql /
for a path at the root,
.Ql \&.
for one with no slash.
.It Fn completions line
The list of names
.Ic Tab
would offer at the end of
.Ar line ,
found as
.Ic Tab
finds them
.Pq see Sx LINE EDITING ,
but nothing is drawn or inserted:
.Ql "completions('cd /us')"
is
.Ql "['/usr/']" .
Names that only hold the word's letters in order come best first;
any others are sorted.
It shows what a
.Ic complete
function makes of a line.
.It Fn defined name
Whether a variable exists.
.It Fn environ
Sorted list of the names of the exported variables.
.It Fn getenv name
Value of the exported variable
.Ar name ,
as a string, or the empty string if there is none.
.It Fn setenv "name, value"
Set the global
.Ar name
to
.Ar value ,
as a string, and export it; a local of that name is left alone.
A name that is empty or holds
.Ql =
is an error.
.It Fn now
Milliseconds since the epoch, for timing:
.Ql "t := now(); make; writeln(now() - t, 'ms')" .
.It Fn time "fmt[, ms]"
The local time, or the instant
.Ar ms
from
.Fn now ,
formatted by
.Ar fmt
as by
.Xr strftime 3 ,
with these conversions:
.Ql \&%Y
and
.Ql \&%y
(year),
.Ql \&%m ,
.Ql \&%d
and
.Ql \&%e
(month and day),
.Ql \&%H ,
.Ql \&%M
and
.Ql \&%S ,
.Ql \&%a
and
.Ql \&%A
(weekday),
.Ql \&%b
and
.Ql \&%B
(month name),
.Ql \&%s
(seconds since the epoch),
.Ql \&%z
and
.Ql \&%Z
(time zone),
.Ql \&%F
.Pq Ql \&%Y-%m-%d ,
.Ql \&%T
.Pq Ql \&%H:%M:%S ,
.Ql \&%R
.Pq Ql \&%H:%M
and
.Ql \&%% .
Any other is an error.
The zone is read once, at the first call, from
.Ev TZ
as the C library reads it: unset,
.Pa /etc/localtime ;
empty, UTC; otherwise a file, a path or a name such as
.Ql Europe/London
under
.Ev TZDIR
or
.Pa /usr/share/zoneinfo ,
a leading
.Ql \&:
dropped; failing that, a POSIX rule such as
.Ql EST5EDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0 ;
and UTC for anything that doesn't read.
.El
.Pp
.Fn getenv
and
.Fn setenv
take the name as a string, so it may be worked out
while the script runs:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
for n in environ() do
  if match(n, 'LC_*') then unset $n
.Ed
.Sh LINE EDITING
When standard input and standard error are terminals and
.Ev TERM
is set and not
.Cm dumb ,
input lines are edited in place.
Lines wider than the terminal wrap.
The prompt may be any width and may contain colour escapes.
.Bl -tag -width "Alt-b, Alt-f, Ctrl-Left, Ctrl-RightXX" -offset indent
.It Ic Left , Right , ^B , ^F
Move one character.
At the end of the line,
.Ic Right
and
.Ic ^F
accept the whole history suggestion.
.It Ic Home , End , ^A , ^E
Start or end of the line.
At the end of the line,
.Ic End
and
.Ic ^E
accept the whole history suggestion.
.It Ic Alt-b , Alt-f , Ctrl-Left , Ctrl-Right
Move one word.
At the end of the line,
.Ic Alt-f
and
.Ic Ctrl-Right
accept the history suggestion up to the end of its next word.
.It Ic Backspace , ^H
Delete the character before the cursor.
.It Ic Delete , ^D
Delete the character under the cursor.
.Ic ^D
on an empty line ends input.
.It Ic ^W , Alt-Backspace
Kill the word before the cursor.
.It Ic Alt-d , Ctrl-Delete
Kill the word after the cursor.
.It Ic ^K
Kill to the end of the line.
.It Ic ^U
Kill to the start of the line.
.It Ic ^Y
Insert the text last killed.
Kills in a row add up, so
.Ic ^W ^W ^Y
puts back two words.
Backspace, Delete and
.Ic ^D
delete without killing.
.It Ic ^_ , ^X^U
Undo the last change to the line; again, the one before.
A run of typing is one change.
.It Ic Alt-. , Alt-_
Insert the last word of the previous history line; again, swap it
for the last word of the line before that.
Quotes keep a word together.
.It Ic Alt-u , Alt-l , Alt-c
Upper-case, lower-case or capitalise the word after the cursor, and
move past it.
Only ASCII letters change.
.It Ic ^T
Swap the two characters before the cursor.
.It Ic Alt-t
Swap the word before the cursor with the word after it, or at the end
of the line, the last two words.
.It Ic ^X^E
Edit the line in
.Ev VISUAL ,
or
.Ev EDITOR ,
or
.Xr vi 1 ,
through a file in
.Pa /tmp .
The editor may be a procedure, and may take options after blanks, as
in
.Ql emacs -nw .
What it leaves is echoed and run, and each of its lines goes into the
history; if it leaves nothing, nothing runs.
An editor stopped with Ctrl-Z, or one that cannot be started, ends the
edit with the line as the file holds it.
.It Ic ^L
Clear the screen and redraw.
.It Ic Up , Down , ^P , ^N
Move through history.
Unsubmitted text is kept when you return to the bottom of the history.
.It Ic ^R
Search history as you type.
A line matches when the letters typed appear in it in order, not
necessarily together, as in completion; the best match comes first,
the newest of equally good ones.
.Ic ^R
again finds the next best,
.Ic Backspace
shortens the query,
.Ic ^G
or
.Ic ^C
cancel,
.Ic Enter
runs the match, and any other key keeps the match and acts normally.
.It Ic Tab
Complete the word before the cursor.
The first word of a command completes to a keyword, a builtin, a
procedure or a program in
.Ev PATH .
In an expression \(em after
.Cm := ,
inside the parentheses of a call or of
.Cm $( ,
or after
.Cm if , while , until , case , to , downto
or
.Cm in
\(em a word completes to a keyword, a builtin routine, a procedure or
a variable.
.Cm $ Ns Ar name
completes to a variable.
An argument of a command first asks a
.Ic complete
function, if there is one (see
.Sx Programmable completion
below); otherwise, then the command's completion file, if it has one
.Pq Xr concha-complete 5 ;
otherwise an argument of
.Ic cd
completes to a directory, and one of
.Ic type
or
.Ic help
as a first word does.
.Cm ~ Ns Ar user
completes to a user's home,
.Cm ~ Ns Ar user Ns Cm / .
Anything else, a string in an expression included, completes to a
file name, with a
.Cm /
after directories.
Spaces and special characters are escaped with
.Cm \e ,
or quoted to match an open string.
If no name starts with the word, names that hold its letters in order
do, as in
.Xr wuzzy 1 :
.Ql dwn
completes to
.Ql Downloads/ ,
and case is ignored unless the word has a capital.
Those come best match first; other names are sorted.
If several names match, their common prefix is inserted; if that adds
nothing, they are shown in a menu below the line.
Hidden files are offered only when the word starts with a dot.
.It Ic Tab , Shift-Tab No in the menu
Select the next or previous name, which stands in for the word.
Once a name is selected, the arrows move around the menu,
.Ic Enter
takes it without running the line, and
.Ic ^G
or
.Ic ^C
puts back the word as typed.
Any other key takes the selection and then acts as usual, so
.Cm /
goes on into a directory.
A menu taller than the screen shows as many rows as fit, around the
selection.
.It Ic ^C
Discard the line.
.It Ic Enter
Run the line.
If the input is unfinished (an open
.Cm begin ,
string or heredoc), a
.Cm ".\ "
prompt asks for more.
.El
.Pp
When the cursor is at the end of a line, the newest history line that
starts with it is suggested: the rest of it is shown dim after the
cursor, to be taken with the keys above or ignored.
Continuation lines get no suggestion.
After a command is not found and the error suggests another, the line
just entered, with that word corrected, is the next line's suggestion,
ahead of history and shown even before anything is typed; Right
then Return runs it.
.Pp
History is kept in
.Pa ~/.concha_history .
Each line is appended when it is entered.
Blank lines and repeats of the previous line are not recorded.
The newest 1000 lines are loaded at startup, and the file is trimmed
when it grows much longer than that.
.Ss Programmable completion
A function named
.Ic complete
completes the arguments of commands.
Its one argument is a list of the command's words up to the cursor,
unquoted, with redirections left out: the first is the command's name
and the last is the word being completed, perhaps empty.
It returns the words that word might become, as a list or as a string
of lines; those that start with the word are offered.
When it returns nothing, the command's completion file is read, and
without one the usual rules apply.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
function complete(words);
begin
  result := [];
  case words[0] of
    'git':
      if length(words) = 2 then
        result := ['add', 'checkout', 'commit', 'diff', 'log', 'status']
      else if words[1] = 'checkout' then
        result := `git branch --format='%(refname:short)'`;
    'make':
      result := `grep -o '^[a-z][^:$=]*:' Makefile 2>/dev/null | tr -d :`
  end
end;
.Ed
.Pp
It runs inside the shell with standard input, output and error on
.Pa /dev/null ,
so nothing it prints reaches the screen, and
.Va status
is kept.
An error in it counts as returning nothing.
The keyboard is not watched while it runs, so it should be quick.
A completion file, named after the program and kept in
.Pa ~/.local/share/concha/complete
or beside the programs installed, describes the program's
subcommands, options and operands, which are then completed without
running anything; its format is in
.Xr concha-complete 5 .
Keep to
.Ic complete
what has to be worked out when
.Ic Tab
is pressed, such as git branches.
.Pp
To see what it makes of a line, call
.Fn completions
with that line:
.Ql "writeln(completions('git checkout '))" .
.Ss Colour
Colour is on when standard error is a terminal,
.Ev TERM
is set and not
.Cm dumb ,
and
.Ev NO_COLOR
is unset or empty.
It uses only the eight basic colours, bold and dim, so the terminal's
own palette applies; there is nothing to configure.
With colour on, errors start with a bold red
.Sq concha: ,
the colour names in
.Sx VARIABLES AND ENVIRONMENT
hold escape sequences, and the line is highlighted as you type:
.Bl -tag -width "single-quoted stringsXX" -offset indent -compact
.It comments
dim
.It single-quoted strings
green
.It double-quoted strings
yellow
.It Cm $ Ns Ar name , Cm $( Ns Ar ... Ns Cm \&) , Cm ${ Ns Ar ... Ns Cm }
cyan
.It keywords
bold, where a statement or expression is expected
.It backticks
magenta
.It the command word
blue if it names a builtin, a procedure or a program that can run,
red if not
.El
.Pp
In completion lists, directories are blue and executable files green.
.Pp
The highlighter sees only the line being edited, so a continuation
line of a string or comment is coloured as if it stood alone.
.Sh JOB CONTROL
In an interactive shell each external command or pipeline is a job: a
process group of its own, which holds the terminal while it runs in
the foreground.
Ctrl-Z stops the job in the foreground; the shell reports
.Dl [1] stopped vi notes
and goes back to the prompt with
.Ev status
148, abandoning any loop it was in, as Ctrl-C does.
A background job that reads from the terminal stops too, and is
reported at the next prompt, together with the jobs that have
finished.
.Pp
Jobs are numbered from 1, reusing numbers that have come free.
The current job is the one most recently stopped or started in the
background.
.Ic fg
and
.Ic bg
take a job's number, with or without a leading
.Sq % ,
and default to the current job:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vi notes
^Z
[1] stopped vi notes
make > log &
[2] 4242 make
jobs
[1]  4239 stopped vi notes
[2]+ 4242 running make
fg 1
.Ed
.Pp
A job that stops keeps its terminal settings, which
.Ic fg
gives back; otherwise the terminal is left as the last command that
exited set it, or reset if the command was killed.
.Ic kill
is an external program and takes process ids, which
.Ic jobs
lists; a negative one names a whole job, as in
.Cm "kill -- -4242" .
Leaving the shell while jobs are stopped takes a second
.Ic exit
or Ctrl-D in a row; the first only says
.Dl concha: there are stopped jobs
The system then sends them
.Dv SIGHUP .
When the shell itself gets
.Dv SIGHUP ,
as when its terminal window closes, it passes the signal on to every
job, running or stopped, then dies of it;
.Ic disown
takes a job out of its reach.
Jobs left running by a plain
.Ic exit
carry on.
.Pp
Scripts have no job control: see
.Sx SIGNALS .
.Sh SIGNALS
The interactive shell ignores
.Dv SIGQUIT ,
.Dv SIGTSTP ,
.Dv SIGTTIN
and
.Dv SIGTTOU .
Any shell, a script too, passes
.Dv SIGHUP
on to its background jobs before dying of it
.Pq Sx JOB CONTROL .
.Dv SIGINT
stops the running command and any loop inside the shell, then returns
to the prompt, or ends a script with status 130.
If the command in the foreground catches the signal and survives it, as
an interpreter or a pager does, the shell carries on instead; for a
pipeline, its last command decides.
Commands start with default signal handling.
In a script, background jobs ignore
.Dv SIGINT ,
and a command that stops is continued, since nothing could resume it.
.Pp
.Ic trap
changes this.
A trapped signal is noted when it arrives, and its procedure runs
before the next statement; while a command runs in the foreground,
that is once it has finished.
The procedure leaves
.Ev status
as it found it.
A trapped
.Dv SIGINT
no longer stops loops.
The
.Cm EXIT
procedure runs once, when a script ends, on
.Ic halt ,
after an error, and when the interactive shell exits; not when a
signal kills the shell.
Child processes, including captures and pipeline stages, do not run
the shell's trap procedures, but signals ignored with
.Cm "trap ''"
stay ignored in them.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
procedure cleanup; begin rm -f $tmp end;
trap cleanup EXIT
.Ed
.Sh QUERIES
.Nm
.Fl q Ar kind Ar script
(for
.Cm words
and
.Cm def ,
.Ar script : Ns Ar line : Ns Ar column )
prints its answer on standard output, one item to a line, fields
separated by a tab, lines and columns counted from 1, columns in
bytes.
The kinds are:
.Bl -tag -width outline
.It Cm check
The first syntax error, as
.Ar line ,
.Ar column
and
.Ar message ,
or nothing if the script parses.
.It Cm outline
Each
.Ic procedure
and
.Ic function ,
nested or not, and each name declared with
.Ic var
or
.Ic export
outside them, in the order they appear:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kind	name	line	column	last
.Ed
.Pp
.Ar kind
is
.Ql proc ,
.Ql func
or
.Ql var ;
.Ar line
and
.Ar column
are where the name is, and
.Ar last
is the line of a routine's closing
.Ic end ,
a variable's own line.
If the script has a syntax error, the outline is of what was parsed
before it, and a routine still open there ends on the error's line.
.It Cm words
What can be typed at
.Ar line
and
.Ar column
of the script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kind	word	detail
.Ed
.Pp
.Ar kind
is
.Ql keyword ,
.Ql builtin
(a builtin command or routine),
.Ql proc ,
.Ql func
or
.Ql var .
.Ar detail
is how a builtin is used, as in
.Sx BUILTIN COMMANDS
and
.Sx BUILTIN ROUTINES ,
and a routine's signature, as
.Ql name(a, b) ;
it is empty for the rest.
The routines are all those of the script.
The variables are the globals declared before that place, the
locals and parameters of the routines it is inside, and, in a
routine, the locals declared before it.
If the script has a syntax error, the words are those of what was
parsed before it, plus the names of the routines on the lines after
it, found by their keyword at the start of a line, with no parameters.
.It Cm def
Where the routine named at
.Ar line
and
.Ar column
of the script is declared:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
line	column	name
.Ed
.Pp
The place may be on a call of a procedure or function of the script,
a command word or a call in an expression, or on its declaration,
which answers itself.
A routine is found wherever it stands in the script, before the
call or after it; when routines of one name are nested, the
innermost declaration visible from the call is the one.
A builtin, an external command, a variable and anything else give
nothing.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width NO_COLOR -offset indent
.It Ev EDITOR
The editor for
.Ic ^X^E
when
.Ev VISUAL
is unset or empty; see
.Sx LINE EDITING .
.It Ev HOME
The directory for
.Cm ~ ,
.Ic cd
with no argument,
.Pa ~/.concharc
and
.Pa ~/.concha_history .
.It Ev NO_COLOR
If set and not empty, no colour is used; see
.Sx Colour .
.It Ev PATH
Where commands are looked for.
If it is unset or empty,
.Pa /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
is searched.
.It Ev TZ
The time zone for
.Fn time ,
as
.Ql Europe/London
or a POSIX rule; read at its first call.
.It Ev TZDIR
Where
.Ev TZ
names a zone file, if not
.Pa /usr/share/zoneinfo .
.It Ev TERM
A value of
.Cm dumb ,
or none, turns off line editing and colour.
.It Ev VISUAL
The editor for
.Ic ^X^E ;
see
.Sx LINE EDITING .
.It Ev XDG_DATA_HOME , XDG_DATA_DIRS
Where completion files are looked for, under
.Pa concha/complete ;
see
.Xr concha-complete 5 .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/.concha_historyX" -offset indent
.It Pa ~/.concharc
Sourced by the interactive shell before the first prompt.
The
.Pa concharc
among the examples
.Pq see Xr concha-tutorial 7
is a commented one to start from.
.It Pa ~/.concha_history
Line-editing history.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/concha/complete/
Completion files, one per program
.Pq Xr concha-complete 5 ;
also
.Pa concha/complete
under
.Ev XDG_DATA_HOME
and each directory of
.Ev XDG_DATA_DIRS .
.It Pa /usr/local/share/concha/examples
Example scripts and a commented
.Pa concharc ,
listed in
.Xr concha-tutorial 7 .
The directory follows the prefix concha was installed with.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The final
.Ev status ,
or the argument of
.Ic halt
or
.Ic exit ;
2 after a syntax or runtime error in a script;
with
.Fl n ,
0 if the script parses and 2 if it does not;
with
.Fl q ,
0 when it answered, even when the script has an error, and 2 for a
bad query: an unknown kind, a missing
.Ar script
or one that cannot be read;
130 after an interrupt;
127 if the script cannot be read.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Errors go to standard error, after the file and line of the statement
that failed:
.Dl concha: lib.cn:12: undefined variable nosuch
The file is the one the statement was read from: the script, a file
run with
.Ic source ,
or
.Pa .concharc ,
wherever the procedure holding it was called from.
A script given with
.Fl c
or read from standard input shows the line alone, and a line typed at
the prompt neither.
With
.Fl n ,
a syntax error's line is followed by its column,
.Ql file:line:col: ,
as compilers and editors expect.
With
.Fl q ,
an error found in the script is the answer, on standard output;
only a bad query goes to standard error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
// count lines of Pascal
var total := 0
for f in glob('*.pas') do
  total := total + int(`wc -l < $f`)
writeln(total, ' lines')

// arguments
case length(args) of
  0: begin echo 'usage: deploy host' >&2; halt(2) end
else
  host := args[0]
end

// a pipeline feeding the shell
var big := []
du -s * | while readln(l) do
  if int(split(l)[0]) > 1000 then big := big + [split(l)[1]]
.Ed
.Pp
The last loop runs in a child process because it is a stage of a
pipeline, so
.Ev big
is left unchanged.
Give the loop its input with a redirection instead to keep the
result:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
begin
  while readln(l) do big := big + [l]
end < list.txt
.Ed
.Sh DIFFERENCES FROM SH
No word splitting, no
.Ev IFS ,
no
.Ic [
or
.Ic test ,
no arithmetic expansion (use
.Cm $( Ns Ar expr Ns Cm \&) ),
no brace expansion, no
.Ic eval ,
no
.Cm >( Ns Ar list Ns Cm \&) ,
and no subshell parentheses.
.Cm ~ Ns Ar user
is looked up in
.Pa /etc/passwd
only, not through
.Xr getpwnam 3 ,
so users from LDAP and the like are not known.
Undefined variables are errors.
A glob that matches nothing is an error.
There are no assignments before a command:
.Cm "FOO=bar cmd"
is the command
.Cm FOO=bar ,
and when it isn't found the error suggests
.Cm "env FOO=bar cmd"
instead.
.Sh BUGS
When editing, a character takes the columns
.Xr wcwidth 3
gives it, two for most CJK characters and emoji; a sequence of
characters joined by zero-width joiners, as some emoji are, counts as
its parts.
Completion guesses what a word is from the line up to the cursor, and
the guess can be wrong.
.Pp
A redirection after a loop's body belongs to the body, not the loop:
.Cm "while readln(l) do cat <<< "
feeds each
.Ic cat
its line, while the loop itself reads standard input.
When the body is a Pascal call, as in
.Cm "while readln(l) do writeln(l) < f" ,
that is almost certainly a mistake, and it is a syntax error; wrap the
loop in
.Cm begin ... end
and redirect that.
With a command as the body it cannot be told from intent, so it is
allowed.
